      },
      "text": "8 January – Lake Titicaca, Uros and Taquile"
    },
    {
      "blockId": "tour:itinerary_text:49b18e3fb3f9ec7b",
      "field": "itinerary.text",
      "sourceTextSha256": "d05bf9559f7ba2ac593e1708dd2338d74ccb739bf56c69fdb770e2dff8938120",
      "provenance": {
        "kind": "ai_translation",
        "runId": "ada-en-worker-20261107-z3b7r3j6r5-yr8z7okdet",
        "model": "GPT-6.1"
      },
      "text": "Breakfast, then transfer to the port.\n\nFor many Peruvians, Lake Titicaca remains sacred. According to legend, Manco Cápac, the first Inca ruler, was a descendant of the Sun and was born in the waters of the lake. The Uros call themselves the ‘people of the lake’.\n\nThe tour starts at the floating reed islands of the Uros. There are more than 40 of them. You’ll learn about the islands’ history, with time to buy souvenirs and take a ride on a reed boat.\n\nYou’ll continue across the lake to Taquile Island. The main village sits high on the island at about **3,950 m**. Residents maintain their own community rules and traditional way of life. There are no police, vehicles, cats or dogs on the island.\n\nTaquile is especially well known for its weaving. In 2005, UNESCO proclaimed this art a ‘Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage of Humanity’.\n\nLunch on the island, then return to Puno.\n\nThe two visits offer different perspectives on life around Lake Titicaca. The Uros visit focuses on the floating islands, built from reeds, and the community’s close ties to the lake. On Taquile, you’ll climb to the upper part of the island to learn about the community’s traditions and crafts."
    },
